<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidon, or Saida, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, about 40 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, inhabited since prehistoric times. One of the most important Phoenician cities, it was known for its glass and purple dye, and under Persian rule it eclipsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> as the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took it in 1110 and made it the seat of the Lordship of Sidon, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was the capital of a province. It is now the third-largest city in Lebanon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidon.jpg" alt="Sidon" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heretiq · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SidonNewCity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
